// FEATURE: a public comment system on a blog post

// STEP 1 - ASSETS:
// - Other users' browsers/sessions (could be hijacked via malicious
//   script injected through a comment)
// - The comments database itself (could be corrupted or exfiltrated)
// - The site's own availability (could be degraded by spam/abuse)

// STEP 2 - ATTACK SURFACE:
// - The comment submission form's text field (genuinely
//   user-controllable input, flowing into the database AND later
//   displayed back to OTHER users' browsers)
// - The comment search/filter parameter, if one exists
// - The rate at which comments can be submitted (no inherent limit yet)

// STEP 3 - THREATS, one per attack-surface point:
// - Comment text field: a malicious user could submit a comment
//   containing a <script> tag that executes in OTHER users' browsers
//   when displayed (lesson 2's exact XSS territory)
// - Comment text field, if used unsafely in a database query: SQL
//   injection (lesson 3's exact territory)
// - Comment submission rate: a malicious actor could submit
//   thousands of spam comments per second, degrading availability
//   for everyone (lesson 5's exact territory)

// STEP 4 - MITIGATIONS, each tied to its SPECIFIC, named threat:
// - XSS threat -> output encoding/sanitization before display (lesson 2)
// - SQL injection threat -> parameterized queries (lesson 3,
//   directly extending Module 18's existing discipline)
// - Spam/abuse threat -> rate limiting on the submission endpoint (lesson 5)

