sections.content:
A complete, Redis-backed rate limiter, directly extending Module 27,
lesson 3's exact stateless rate-limiting pattern with a genuine,
production-appropriate response:

async function rateLimitMiddleware(req: Request, res: Response, next: NextFunction) {
  const key = `ratelimit:${req.ip}`;
  const count = await redisClient.incr(key);

  if (count === 1) {
    await redisClient.expire(key, 60);
  }

  res.setHeader('X-RateLimit-Limit', '100');
  res.setHeader('X-RateLimit-Remaining', Math.max(0, 100 - count).toString());

  if (count > 100) {
    return res.status(429).json({ success: false, error: 'Too many requests' });
  }
  next();
}

WHY THIS ALONE CANNOT STOP A GENUINE, LARGE-SCALE DDoS ATTACK: each
of 10,000 distinct attacking IPs would EACH individually stay under
the 100-request limit, yet their COMBINED volume (up to 1,000,000
requests) could still genuinely overwhelm the application server
layer entirely - genuine DDoS protection requires infrastructure
operating BEFORE traffic even reaches this application-level code at
all (a dedicated DDoS-mitigation service or CDN-layer protection,
often provided by the same infrastructure providers from Module 25)

sections.code:
// LAYER 1 - Infrastructure-level DDoS protection, operating BEFORE
// traffic ever reaches this application's own code at all (typically
// configured at the CDN/load-balancer level, Module 27 lesson 4's
// exact infrastructure, NOT application code) - absorbs and filters
// the genuinely massive, aggregate traffic volume a true DDoS
// attack produces, well before it could overwhelm individual servers

// LAYER 2 - Application-level rate limiting, handling what it
// GENUINELY can: a single client (by IP, or more precisely by
// authenticated user account) making excessive requests
async function rateLimitMiddleware(req: Request, res: Response, next: NextFunction) {
  const identifier = req.userId ?? req.ip; // prefer the authenticated
    // user's own ID when available - genuinely harder for an
    // attacker to rotate than a raw IP address
  const key = `ratelimit:${identifier}`;
  const count = await redisClient.incr(key);

  if (count === 1) await redisClient.expire(key, 60);

  if (count > 100) {
    return res.status(429).json({ success: false, error: 'Too many requests' });
  }
  next();
}

// LAYER 3 - Stricter, ENDPOINT-SPECIFIC limits for genuinely
// sensitive operations (login attempts, password resets), directly
// extending lesson 4's authentication-security concerns with a
// tighter, more conservative limit than general API traffic
const loginRateLimiter = createRateLimiter({ windowSeconds: 900, maxAttempts: 5 });
app.post('/login', loginRateLimiter, async (req, res, next) => { /* ... */ });

